WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:07.660
Hey, this is super interesting because it's
a sort of perfect blend of political propaganda,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.429
but also of Donald Trump's fragile psychological
state.

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:18.279
As we all know, Donald Trump was woefully
late to the Corona virus preparedness game.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:20.820
He was behind on taking it seriously.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.349
He was behind on procuring supplies.

00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:25.259
He was behind on shutting things down.

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:26.480
He's just been behind.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.060
We had our first case on January 20th.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.450
South Korea had their first case on January
19th.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.190
Essentially the same day.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.760
And yet South Korea immediately developed
a test.

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:39.800
Isolated people did mass testing quarantine.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.789
People did contact tracing.

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:45.620
They still don't have 200 deaths from the
virus.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.070
Meanwhile, we had over 900 deaths just yesterday.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.920
Part of it is because Donald Trump has failed
us.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.199
But here's the psychological part.

00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:58.150
Donald Trump always wants to blame someone
else.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:00:59.870
And in fact, he's been asked a number of times.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.129
Do you take responsibility for this or that?

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:03.820
No, I don't take responsibility.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.230
He has told us.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:12.000
But in order to blame someone else for the
coronavirus response, he's implicitly admitting

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.240
that the response was slow or inadequate.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.601
So yesterday, during the White House coronavirus
press briefing, a reporter asked Donald Trump

00:01:19.601 --> 00:01:22.619
a brilliant question from a psychological
perspective.

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:26.790
And it's brilliant because it was it really
baited Trump into having to make a decision

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:27.790
on the spot.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:34.340
The reporter asked, did impeachment back in
January distract you from Corona virus?

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:36.810
Now, I'm going to play Trump's answer for
you.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.050
But what happens is amazing.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.050
Again, from.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:44.299
From the point of view of analyzing Trump's
psyche, Trump gets this look on his face where

00:01:44.299 --> 00:01:48.700
it's almost like a light bulb goes off or
he says, hey, wait a second, I could blame

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:49.930
Democrats for this.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.590
I could say, yeah, that's right.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.840
The phony impeachment hoax did distract me
from Corona virus.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.320
It's the Dems fault, except if Trump does
that, he's implicitly admitting he was distracted.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:09.450
He's he's admitting by default that the response
was not what it could have been.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.660
He can blame Democrats.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.100
He's been given a get out of jail free card
here.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:18.210
But to do that, he has to admit that he didn't
have the best response.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.090
And ultimately, Trump does admit that he was
distracted by impeachment.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.980
Now, whether it's true or not, we don't we
don't really know, kind of doubt it.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.920
But Trump is calculating in real time.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.510
Hey, wait a second.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.740
Maybe I can say, OK, fine, maybe there is.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:34.520
Bonds wasn't good, but it was because of Democrats.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.520
Check it out today.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.180
He's on the path to Mitch McConnell.

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.180
Yes.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.530
He said that impeachment diverted the attention
of the governor.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.790
Do you think that in any way this was happening
and building at the same time?

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:49.440
Did it divert your attention or your team's
attention or the vise president's attention?

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.170
Well, I don't like to think I did.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:52.590
I think I handled it very well.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.170
But I guess it probably did.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.750
I mean, I got impeached.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:03:00.070
I think, you know, I certainly devoted a little
time to thinking about it.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:01.070
Right.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:02.650
So but think of it.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:03.650
It was a hoax.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.010
It was a total hoax.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.740
And when you think that I got impeached only
because they had a majority in the House,

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.120
they didn't get one.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.430
They did get one Republican vote.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:15.030
One hundred and ninety six to nothing.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:16.260
Not one Republican step.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.630
I don't think it's ever happened.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:23.290
The Republicans stuck together and they stuck
together in the Senate 52 to hash.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:27.760
This just brilliantly twists Trump into a
sort of pretzel.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.791
And later he says, Well, even though I was
distracted, I don't really know that I could

00:03:31.791 --> 00:03:32.880
have done anything differently.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.030
He's saying, yeah, well, sort of.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:36.500
I was distracted.

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:39.120
I was impeached and it was a hoax.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.210
And Democrats are bad.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:43.040
And it's Democrats that affected my response.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.730
But still, my response was as good as it could
have been.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:48.440
And I couldn't have done anything more quickly.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:52.870
Trump's actual andthe answer is worthless
here as a factual inquiry.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:58.610
But it is gold as a window into Trump's psyche,
which is the light bulb goes off and he gets

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:03.420
this idea, hey, wait a second, I can blame
Democrats for this.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.910
And Trump, of course, wants to do that.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:12.010
But he still has to argue that despite being
distracted by impeachment, he wasn't distracted

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:16.360
enough to do anything wrong on Corona virus
response.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.360
It's incredible.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.540
And it is quite a window into Donald Trump's
desperation to figure out how can he untwist

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:28.610
himself from this intractable situation where
he was slow.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.540
The response was a joke.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.700
His communications have been disproportionately
propaganda.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:40.280
Every once in a while with some kind of action
item sprinkled in there and an excellent job

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.950
by the reporter whose name escapes me.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:42.950
And I apologize.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:45.840
I really should be naming him because he did
a really good job there.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.790
A very, very interesting.

